{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 35, :comments ["The reason for <b>and your brother shall live with you</b> is that he shall live with you, and it is a positive commandment to keep him alive, for from it we were commanded concerning preservation of life by a positive commandment. From here they said (Sifra, chapter 5:3), \"'And your brother shall live with you' — this is what Ben Petori derived: two who were walking on the road and one of them had a flask of water — if one drinks he reaches civilization, but if both drink both die. Ben Petori derived: it is better that both drink and die, and that one not witness the death of his fellow.\" Until Rabbi Akiva came and taught: \"'And your brother shall live with you' — your life takes precedence over the life of your fellow.\" And He returned and said (Leviticus 25:36), \"and your brother shall live with you,\" to strengthen and to warn. And its midrash (Bava Metzia 62a): he must return to him the interest deducted from the principal — for the verse commands restoration of interest that was taken, as it says concerning robbery (Leviticus 5:23), \"and he shall restore the stolen thing that he stole.\" And Onkelos (Onkelos on Leviticus 25:35) rendered \"ger and toshav and he shall live with you\" — all of it as part of the commandment: \"ואתדור ויתותב ויחי עמך\" (you shall dwell and be settled and live with Me). But according to the view of our Sages in the Gemara (Bava Metzia 71a): \"and you shall strengthen him\" — the stranger and the settler, \"and your brother shall live with you\" — the life of each one of them is with you."], :book :leviticus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 25, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T04:00:36.075441112Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}